---
title: Mitigation Lifecycle
config:
    layout: elk
---
stateDiagram-v2
    direction TB

    [*] --> ready_for_rollback: from process incident-response (IC requests rollback)
    [*] --> ready_for_flag_toggle: from process incident-response (IC requests flag toggle)
    [*] --> ready_for_hotfix: from process incident-response (IC spawns hotfix issue)

    state "Rollback" as rollback_group {
        ready_for_rollback --> rolling_back: responder claims rollback
        rolling_back --> rolled_back: responder completes rollback
    }

    state "Flag Toggle" as flag_toggle_group {
        ready_for_flag_toggle --> toggling_flag: responder claims flag toggle
        toggling_flag --> flag_toggled: responder completes toggle
    }

    ready_for_hotfix --> [*]: to process inner-loop

    rolled_back --> [*]: terminal (shipped) — returns to incident verification
    flag_toggled --> [*]: terminal (shipped) — returns to incident verification

    note right of ready_for_rollback
        Cross-process legend:
        • Entry spawn events: all three ready_for_* states are spawned by incident-response when the IC picks a mitigation strategy. Each is a new work item.
        • Exit shared state: ready_for_hotfix (to inner-loop). The hotfix issue is the work item; inner-loop picks up the same item from this queue.
        • Exit external: rolled_back / flag_toggled terminate this diagram and signal incident-response to resume verification.

        Urgent queue — responder claim SLA is minutes. Distinct from release-lifecycle's rolling_back: that is a release-level rollback of a specific release tag. This rollback is an incident mitigation (may be the same operation under the hood).
    end note

    note right of ready_for_flag_toggle
        Urgent queue — responder claim SLA is minutes. Tracks the act of flipping a flag as a mitigation, not the flag's runtime state.
    end note

    note right of ready_for_hotfix
        Shared resting state with inner-loop. Hotfix is a new issue spawned by the IC. It skips refinement (scope is defined on creation) and is developed on the inner-loop diagram, then shipped on the release diagram as a single-issue patch train.

        This diagram does not track the hotfix work; once inner-loop claims, mitigation's responsibility for the hotfix ends. Incident-response resumes when the hotfix production deploy is confirmed (see incident-response-lifecycle).
    end note

    note right of rolled_back
        If the hotfix path is taken instead and must be backported to an older release branch, see backport-lifecycle. Backport is a separate work item spawned from the hotfix, not tracked here.
    end note
